From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the block allocator's large runs by their size

The block allocator kept every free run of 63 slots or more in one
list, and a request for one passed at most 32 runs too small before it
took a new chunk. Collections run seldom while the shell leaves room
for its openings, so the list filled with runs of 63 to 127 slots at
its head, and requests passed them while runs that fit sat further on.
Over 130 openings in a lab the shell's live heap stayed at 18 MB while
it held 25 MB growing to 40, 12 MB of it free in that one list. Runs
of 63 to 127 slots now share the first of five large bins, and each
bin after it holds runs from a power of two to the next, the last
every run of 1024 slots or more. A request passes at most 32 runs too
small in its own bin, then takes the first run of a larger one, which
always fits. It takes a new chunk only when no larger bin holds a run
and its own holds none that fits within those 32. The same lab held
25 MB growing to 34.

---
